                "visible_text_excerpt": "The Tribe Network Skip to content The Tribe Network Skip to main content Free to join The Tribe Get practical support MEN • COMMUNITY • DIRECTION • PRACTICAL SUPPORT THE TRIBE NETWORK A community of men finding direction together. Belong. Speak freely. Share what you know. Learn from other men. Find your direction — and practical help when you need it. A place for adult men to connect through real conversation, useful knowledge, interests, practical experience and perspectives worth exploring. Free to join The Tribe Get practical support One community. Useful knowledge. Real interests. Practical help when discussion is not enough. BelongSpeak freelyShare what you knowLearn from other men WHY THE TRIBE NETWORK EXISTS Experience should travel. Every generation of men faces its own challenges. For much of human life, experience travelled between men — through fathers, brothers, friends, mentors and older men who had already walked part of the road. The Tribe Network exists to rebuild some of that connection in a modern, organised place. Why The Tribe exists A place to speak openly, think independently and become more capable in the lives we lead. Share experience. Learn from different perspectives. Explore useful ideas. Pass on practical knowledge. Ask for help when you need it. Not a hive mind. Not one man’s answer. A community of men finding direction together. THE TRIBE IN MOTION Ideas to return to. A changing glimpse of how the network thinks, learns and talks. PrincipleNo single book. No single philosophy. No final answer.Explore it. Question it. Compare it. Decide what is useful. Independent thoughtThink for yourself.Step outside the noise. Better questions create better judgement. Community cultureSpeak freely. Challenge honestly. Respect the tribe.Honest disagreement can make the room stronger. Community cultureChallenge the idea. Respect the man. Protect the room.Different views are welcome. Destructive behaviour is not the price of honesty. BoundaryMen’s issues do not require an enemy.A men’s community can be confident about men without being hostile to anybody else. PerspectiveCuriosity before ideology.Difficult subjects deserve intelligent conversation, not compulsory agreement. ExperienceExperience should travel.Men should be able to learn from men who have already walked part of the road. Personal growthStrength doesn’t mean emotionless.Sometimes strength is acting. Sometimes waiting. Sometimes asking for help. Intergenerational learningNot everybody knows everything. Everybody may know something worth passing on.Experience is perspective, not unquestionable authority. Founder philosophyBuilt from curiosity, not certainty.The aim is not to build another ideology. Community valueDiscover. Discuss. Contribute. Return.A useful community gives men reasons to come back. StandingCommunity standing is earned, not bought.Useful contribution and judgement matter more than status for sale. Resource integrityCommunity-shared does not mean automatically endorsed.Useful material can be explored without treating every source as doctrine. The Reading RoomBooks, articles and talks worth exploring.Good material gives men something to think about, even when they disagree with parts of it. Different VoicesChange the angle.Videos, interviews and podcasts can add context or challenge an assumption. The Men’s TableReal conversation about real life.Work, money, relationships, fatherhood, health, pressure, purpose and starting again. The WorkshopUseful knowledge is often learned by doing.DIY, repairs, practical skills, tools, projects and experience worth passing on. Off the ClockMen are more than their problems.Sport, cars, motorbikes, fishing, outdoors, gaming, technology and the things we simply enjoy. Field NotesLessons learned the hard way should not disappear.What worked, what did not and what somebody wishes they had understood earlier. PreviousPauseNext WHAT YOU’LL FIND INSIDE Conversation, interests and practical knowledge. The network should grow from genuine member demand. These are some of the places men may find common ground. Fatherhood & relationships Work & money Men’s health & wellbeing Books, ideas & culture Cars, DIY & practical skills Sport, fishing, outdoors & interests Pause spotlight Community standing is earned, not bought. The fuller set of rooms and interests belongs inside the network rather than turning Home into a catalogue. THE KNOWLEDGE SHELF Help The Tribe build the shelf. Books, YouTube videos, talks, articles, and ideas worth discussing. These are starting points. If something has helped you think and grow, bring it in. BOOKRobert Greene The 33 Strategies of War Not every conflict deserves a fight. Greene draws on military history to explore when to take the initiative, hold your position, negotiate or walk away, and how to keep a clear head while others react. Why The Tribe thinks it matters: A strong framework for thinking about conflict, timing, initiative, positioning, defence, negotiation and when not to fight.Founder’s takeawayUnderstand the terrain, control the pace, choose battles carefully, keep the initiative where useful, and avoid reactive or unwinnable moves. StrategySource ↗ BOOKRobert Greene The 48 Laws of Power Who holds the power, and how are they using it? Greene’s deliberately provocative laws examine influence, reputation and manipulation, offering a way to recognise the game without having to adopt every tactic. Why The Tribe thinks it matters: Useful as a map of power dynamics, status, reputation, incentives, influence and manipulation, especially for recognising what other people may be doing rather than copying every law.Founder’s takeawayPower often works indirectly. Before reacting, understand incentives, status, reputation, timing, leverage and possible manipulation. Human behaviourSource ↗ AUDIOBOOKNiccolò Machiavelli The Prince & The Art of War: The Definitive Collection on Statecraft and Military Tactics What keeps a ruler in power when ideals meet hard reality? These two works by Machiavelli examine political survival, leadership and military organisation, inviting you to question the distance between appearances and motives. Why The Tribe thinks it matters: A useful historical lens on power, statecraft, competition and human motives.Founder’s takeawayLook beneath appearances. Understand motives, incentives, power, reputation and the terrain before deciding how to act. StrategySource ↗ AUDIOBOOKAlan Watts Out of Your Mind: Essential Listening from the Alan Watts Audio Archives What happens when you question the identity and assumptions you take for granted? In these recorded talks, Watts draws on Eastern and Western traditions to challenge habitual thinking about the self and its place in the world. Why The Tribe thinks it matters: A perspective-shifting way to examine assumptions and look at familiar problems from another angle.Founder’s takeawaySometimes clarity comes from changing the frame: question the assumptions occupying your attention and look at the situation from another angle. PerspectiveSource ↗ AUDIOBOOKArnold Schwarzenegger Be Useful: Seven Tools for Life A clear vision is only the beginning. Schwarzenegger sets out seven practical tools for turning ambition into effort, learning from setbacks and making yourself useful to other people. Why The Tribe thinks it matters: Its emphasis on hard work, grit, reinvention, clear thinking and continuing to move when circumstances are difficult.Founder’s takeawayProgress usually requires useful action, sustained effort and resilience rather than waiting for ideal conditions. DisciplineSource ↗ AUDIOBOOKPaul Mcgee How Not to Worry: The Remarkable Truth of How a Small Change Can Help You Stress Less and Enjoy Life More When worry takes over, even a small problem can fill the room. McGee explores the thinking habits that keep it going and practical ways to regain perspective and move towards a constructive response. Why The Tribe thinks it matter",
